<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-18" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Double Integrals</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now turn our intentions to integration. Recall from Calculus II (Math 152) how we used integral to calculate the area between a function and the <m>x</m>-axis.
      We took an area like this:
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>Area under curve</caption>
      <image source="assets/integral1.jpg" width="60%">
        <description>
        <p>
        A figure showing the area between a curve and the x-axis shaded and labeled as A1 (positive area) and A2 (negative area)
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  and using rectangles to estimate the area. As we increase the number of rectangles, we improve the estimate and when we let the number of integral approach infinity
  (calculus!) we get the exact area that we call the (definite) integral.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <caption>Area under curve</caption>
      <image source="assets/integral2.jpg" width="60%">
        <description>
        <p>
        A figure showing are between a curve and the x-axis approximated by seven rectangles.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  We will now see how this idea can be extended to three dimensions
</p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>Double Integrals</title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculating double integrals over rectangles
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Iterated integrals and their properties
        </p>
      </li>
      
          </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-double-integrals-rectangles">
    <title>Double Integrals over Rectangles</title>
   
    <p>
      In three dimensions, instead of calculating area under the curve, we calculate the volume between a surface and the <m>xy-</m>plane. In a similar way to area in
      Math 152, we can divide a rectangle in the <m>xy-</m>plane into smaller rectangle and use these as the base for a box. The height of the box is found using a
      <m>z=f(x,y)</m> value. If we add up the area of all of the boxes, we get an estimate for the volume. Here is an example of the volume under the surface
      <m>z=1-0.2x^2+0.2y^2</m> with 100 boxes to estimate the volume.
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>Area under curve</caption>
      <image source="assets/boxes.png" width="60%">
        <description>
        <p>
        A figure showing volume being estimated by boxes under surface.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  Similar to area, volume below the <m>xy-</m>plane is negative due to the negative "height". When we increase the number of boxes toward infinity (calculus again!) we 
  get the exact area between a surface and the <m>xy</m>-plane. This sets up a double integral.
  <md>
    \int_c^d \int_a^b f(x,y)dx dy
  </md>
  The small rectangles that form the bases of the boxes in the <m>xy-</m>plane have sides <m>dx</m> and <m>dy</m> and area <m>dx dy</m>. The function, <m>f(x,y)</m> provides
  a height, so the double integral by definition calculates a volume. Similar to partial derivatives, double integrals are calculated using antiderivatives one variable 
  at a time while holding the second variable as a constant. We work from the inside out and plug in the limits of integration to evaluate the integral and get our
  final result (Fundamental Theorem of Calculus). Here is an example 
</p>
<example>
  <p>
    Evaluate
    <md>
      \int_{-1}^1 \int_{-2}^2 (2x+3y+5) dx dy
    </md>
    Start by calculating the inside integral with respect to <m>x</m> while holding <m>y</m> as a constant
    <md>
      \int_{-1}^1 \left.(x^2 + 3xy + 5x)\right\vert_{-2}^2 dy = \int_{-1}^1 (10+6y) dy
    </md>
    Now calculate the integral with respect to <m>y</m>.
    <md>
      \int_{-1}^1 (10+6y) dy = \left.(10y+3y^2)\right\vert_{-2}^2 = 40
    </md>
  </p>
</example>
<p>
  This process of integrating one variable at a time is also called iterated integration and is always calculated starting with the inner most integral. This will be 
  extended to triple integrals in Calculus IV. Here is a video with a few more examples
</p>
<figure>
    <caption>Double Integrals (25 minutes)</caption>
    <video youtube="BJ_0FURo9RE"/>
  </figure>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Reading Check-in</title>
    <p>
      Check our WAMAP course page for a few questions that go along with today's reading on double integrals.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-iterated-integrals">
    <title>Iterated Integrals</title>
    <p>
      Iterated Integrals have one important property known as Fubini's Theorem. For a rectangular region in the <m>xy-</m>plane <m>[a,b]\times[c,d]</m>,
      <md>
        \int_c^d \int_a^b f(x,y)dx dy = \int_a^b \int_c^d f(x,y)dy dx
      </md>
      In other words, for rectangular regions only, you can switch the order of integration and get the same result. The situation is a little bit more complicated for 
      regions that are now rectangles, and we will look at this in the next section.

    </p>
    <p>
      Another more specialized property that we will occasionally use is when the multi-variable function can be written as product of single-variable functions. 
      If <m>f(x,y) = g(x)\cdot h(y)</m> then
      <md>
        \int_c^d \int_a^b f(x,y)dx dy \left(\int_a^b g(x) dx\right)\cdot \left(\int_c^d h(y) dx\right)
      </md>
      Here are a few more examples
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Evaluate
        <md>
        \int_2^4\int_1^9 ye^x dy dx
        </md>
      Here the multi-variable function is the product of functions of each variable so
      <md>
        \int_2^4\int_1^9 ye^x dy dx = \left(\int_2^4 e^x dx \right)\left(\int_1^9 y dy\right) = \left(\left. e^x \right\vert_2^4 \right)\left(\left.\frac{y^2}{2}\right\vert_1^9\right)
      </md>
      <md>
        =(e^4-e^2)\left(\frac{81}{2}-\frac{1}{2}\right) = 40(e^4-e^2)
      </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Evaluate 
        <md>
          \int_0^4\int_0^3 \frac{dx dy}{\sqrt{3x+4y}}
        </md>
        Here we begin be integrating with respect to <m>x</m> since <m>x</m> is the "inside" variable (<m>dx</m> is to the left of <m>dy</m>).
        <md>
          \int_0^4\int_0^3 \frac{dx dy}{\sqrt{3x+4y}} = \int_0^4 \left(\left.\frac{2(3x+4y)^{1/2}}{3}\right\vert_0^3\right) = \frac{2}{3}\int_0^4 (\sqrt{4y+9}-\sqrt{4y}) dy
        </md>
        Now calculate the integral with respect to <m>y</m>
        <md>
          \frac{2}{3}\int_0^4 (\sqrt{4y+9}-\sqrt{4y}) dy = \left(\left.\frac{2}{3}\left( \frac{2}{3}\frac{(4y+9)^{3/2}}{4} -
           \frac{2}{3}\frac{(4y)^{3/2}}{4}\right\vert_0^4\right)\right) = \frac{34}{9}
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the volume under the surface <m>f(x,y)=xe^{xy}</m> over the region <m>[0,1]\times[0,\ln(5)]</m>. Since we get to choose the order of integration, let's think
        about this order is easier if we calculated
        <md>
          \int_0^1 \int_0^{\ln(5)} xe^{xy} dy dx
        </md>
        we would begin by substituting <m>s=xy</m> and <m>ds = xdy \,\,\to\,\, \frac{ds}{x} = dy</m> which would cancel out the <m>x</m> in front of the exponential.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we instead calculate 
        <md>
        \int_0^{\ln(5)}\int_0^1  xe^{xy}  dx dy
        </md>
        we would begin by using integration by parts with the variable <m>x</m>. This would turn into a complicated mess. So let's proceed with the original order.
      </p>
      <p>
        Making the substitution <m>s=xy</m>
         <md>
          \int_0^1 \int_{0}^{\ln(5)} xe^{xy} dy dx = \int_0^1 \int_{s=0}^{s=x\ln(5)} e^s ds dx = \int_0^1 (e^s\vert_{0}^{x\ln(5)})dx = \int_0^1 (e^{x\ln(5)}-1) dx
        </md>
        Calculate the integral with respect to <m>x</m> now
        <md>
          \int_0^1 (e^{x\ln(5)}-1) dx = \int_0^1 (e^{\ln(5^x)}-1) dx = \int_0^1 (5^x-1) dx = \left.\left(\frac{5^x}{\ln(5)} - x\right)\right\vert_0^1 = \frac{4}{\ln(5)}-1
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      And here is a video with one final example:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>An iterated integral example (9 minutes)</caption>
      <video youtube="IO080wDQq8U"/>
    </figure>
